WEBVTT

gender-04-1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:04.660
Last time: giving birth was treated as an
IOU.

gender-04-2
00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:06.910
“I chose this freely.”

gender-04-3
00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:11.130
Does that cancel the debt?

gender-04-4
00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:18.080
Imagine two women making the same choice:
leaving work to care for their families

gender-04-5
00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:18.790
full-time.

gender-04-6
00:00:18.790 --> 00:00:21.040
“I chose this freely.”

gender-04-7
00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:25.020
Let's begin by believing them.

gender-04-8
00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:28.720
Savings: in her own name.

gender-04-9
00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:31.730
Her former field: she can return anytime.

gender-04-10
00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:35.130
Her partner: willing to take turns
providing care.

gender-04-11
00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:40.230
If she wants to return to work tomorrow,
the door is open.

gender-04-12
00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:43.720
Savings: in her husband's name.

gender-04-13
00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:47.570
Her former field: hard to return after a
three-year gap.

gender-04-14
00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:51.070
Her partner: “Didn't you choose this?”

gender-04-15
00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:56.160
If she wants to return tomorrow, the door
is closed.

gender-04-16
00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:00.310
The same choice, different degrees of
freedom.

gender-04-17
00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:06.110
The difference is not whether she wants
it, but whether she can leave.

gender-04-18
00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:10.320
“Choosing freely” has at least three
layers:

gender-04-19
00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.530
Willingness: does she want to?

gender-04-20
00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:15.790
Options: what else can she choose?

gender-04-21
00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:20.300
Exit costs: what does changing her mind
cost?

gender-04-22
00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:25.410
“I chose this freely” answers only the
first.

gender-04-23
00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:33.205
Political theorist Michaele Ferguson
criticizes “choice feminism” for the fear

gender-04-24
00:01:33.205 --> 00:01:34.980
of politics behind it.

gender-04-25
00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:42.100
Refusing to judge any choice also accepts
the existing order in which choices are

gender-04-26
00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:42.540
made.

gender-04-27
00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:46.960
“Respect her choice” can mean two things.

gender-04-28
00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:49.790
One: I will not decide for you.

gender-04-29
00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:54.000
The other: you chose it; face the
consequences alone.

gender-04-30
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:59.710
The second sounds respectful, but shuts
the door.

gender-04-31
00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:04.030
This does not mean they lack judgment.

gender-04-32
00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:10.010
Nor that we know better than she does
what she “really” wants.

gender-04-33
00:02:10.010 --> 00:02:18.010
We are not judging the choice itself. We
ask what conditions shaped it.

gender-04-34
00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:22.240
What if the conditions changed?

gender-04-35
00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:25.340
Savings: in her own name.

gender-04-36
00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:29.060
Her former field: no penalty for a career
break.

gender-04-37
00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:33.010
Her partner: taking turns providing care
is no favor.

gender-04-38
00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:36.020
She might still choose to stay home.

gender-04-39
00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:41.340
But then, “I chose this freely” would
truly mean something.

gender-04-40
00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:47.456
Respecting someone's choice includes
asking whether she has the means to

gender-04-41
00:02:47.456 --> 00:02:48.640
choose otherwise.

gender-04-42
00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:56.279
A choice's freedom is measured by the
cost of leaving, not how willingly

gender-04-43
00:02:56.279 --> 00:02:57.870
someone enters.

gender-04-44
00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:01.810
Next time, we keep asking:

gender-04-45
00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:07.680
He loves women. Why might he still be a
misogynist?

gender-04-46
00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:14.890
This is UjiBricks.
